=== (C) The owner's closure of the branch: the integer endgame ===
  Chain (all steps proved in draft 18.1): G25(a),(b) + F1 mass + injectivity
  give  (eta_p-1)(eta_q-1) >= 7 + e_p + e_q  with eta_x = |H & B_x|,
  e_x = #edges inside B_x.  G25(c)+(d)+G22 give hub degree <= 5 in B_p u B_q,
  hence beta_q <= delta_b <= 4 and beta_p <= 4, so eta_p, eta_q <= 4.
  exhaustive integer sweep over 0<=eta_p,eta_q<=4, 0<=e_p,e_q<=4: 6 solution(s): [(4, 4, 0, 0), (4, 4, 0, 1), (4, 4, 0, 2), (4, 4, 1, 0), (4, 4, 1, 1), (4, 4, 2, 0)]
  => eta_p = eta_q = 4 in EVERY solution (e_p+e_q <= 2 still free here;
     it is pinned to 0 by the separate degree step below).
  => beta_p = eta_p = 4, beta_q = eta_q = 4: every B-vertex is a hub,
     d(p) = d(q) = 6, a(p) = a(q) = 5.
  degree step: for a hub b in B_p, G25(c) gives delta_b >= beta_q = 4 and
     G25(c)+(d)+G22 give d(b) <= 5, while d(b) = 1 + eps_b + delta_b:
     integer sweep over (eps_b, delta_b): [(0, 4)]
     => eps_b = 0, delta_b = 4, d(b) = 5 for every b in B_p u B_q, hence
        e_p = e_q = 0 (all of B_p, B_q are hubs since beta = eta).
     |D| = sum_b delta_b = 16, n = 3+4+4+16 = 27
     p,q are hubs => ecc = 2 => every y in D has BOTH parents; injectivity
     then makes y -> (b,c) a BIJECTION D -> B_p x B_q.
  Final step: fix y in D with B_p-parent b1.  Each of the other 3 hubs
  b2 in B_p has ecc(b2)=2, and (G25(a) + e_p=0) its only possible common
  neighbour with y lies in N(y) & D, with a UNIQUE B_p-parent b2 -- so y
  needs >= 3 distinct D-neighbours:
     d(y) >= 2 + 3 = 5  =>  a(y) >= ceil(5/2) = 3 >= 3
     so y is a HUB at distance 3 from u, contradicting H <= ball_2(u) (R3b).
  => THE TRIANGULAR-ENDPOINT BRANCH IS EMPTY.  D6a and D6b both closed.
  Note: the chain never uses the branch hypothesis (TRI) itself, only the
  existence of ONE vertex u with a(u)=1, d(u)=2, ecc(u)=3.  Hence the
  stronger statement: in RES(b) ^ (C6) NO vertex of eccentricity 3 has a=1,
  i.e. EVERY distance-3 pair has both a-values equal to 2.

=== (D) Control-case audit (the new chain must not 'prove' these impossible) ===
  Petersen  n=10  diam=2 rad=2 l=3.000 triangular-endpoint carriers=0
  HS        n=50  diam=2 rad=2 l=7.000 triangular-endpoint carriers=0
  HS+leaf   n=51  diam=3 rad=2 l=6.902 triangular-endpoint carriers=0
  Petersen/HS: diam 2, so no distance-3 pair exists -- the chain never starts
  (first failing step = the setup, exactly as required).  HS+leaf has diam 3
  and l>3 but its ecc-3 vertex is a LEAF (a=1, d=1), excluded by G19/F4, and
  it violates (R3b) anyway (it is the round-5 RES(a)-witness).
  HS+leaf a=1 ecc-3 vertices: [50], degrees [1] => all leaves, G19 applies.

=== (E) Claim Q6.1 (the LAST pocket-1 item): the round-7 narrowing ===
  Frame: induced C6 Z = (u,u1,u2,z,y,x) at positions 0..5 (G13), slots of G21:
  u1',u2' (partners), A=(u1,u2), B=(y,x), P=(u1,y), Q=(u2,x), h0=(no Z-nbr).
  E1  Z is an induced, C4-free 6-cycle.
  E2  G27: (h0,u1',u,x,y) is an induced P5 with endpoint h0  =>  d(h0) <= 5 by G22.
      (G21(iv) forces h0 ~ u1'; u1' has no other Z-neighbour by G15(b).)
      => 9 of G21's 11 slots are now degree-bounded (was 8, Cor G22.1).
  E3  G29: (P,u1,u,x,B), (P,u1,u,x,Q) and (P,y,z,u2,A) are induced P5's with
      endpoint P, so d(P) >= 6 forces P ~ A, P ~ B and P ~ Q (whichever exist).
  E4  C4-freeness then gives: P~A and P~Q are incompatible (apex u2), P~B and
      P~Q are incompatible (apex x), and P,Q are never adjacent to u1' or u2'.
      => d(P) >= 6 AND Q present  ==>  slots A and B are EMPTY, and then
         N(x) = {u,y,Q}, N(y) = {z,x,P}, so a(x) = a(y) = 3 (not 4).
  E5  leaf identity ecc(leaf) = 1 + ecc(neighbour): 7656 instances, 0 failures
      With G26 (a=1 => ecc <= 2) and rad = 2 this forces: RES(b) ^ (C6) has NO
      LEAF at all -- min degree >= 2 -- and every a=1 vertex w has ecc(w) = 2,
      so G20 at w gives the rigid identity n = d(P) + d(w') - 1 for its triangle.
  E6  G30: (w,P,u1,u,x) is an induced P5 for every off-Z neighbour w of P, and a
      neighbour of w outside N(P) u N(u1) u N(u) u N(x) extends it to an induced
      P6 -- forbidden by (C6).  Hence N(w) is confined to {P,u1',A,B,Q} u W, at
      most one w per apex (C4 through P), and N(P) induces a matching, so every
      remaining w has d(w) = 2 with N(w) = {P,w'} a clique: a(w) = 1.  By G26
      ecc(w) = 2, so w needs a neighbour in N(u) = {u1,u1',x}; but w' is off-Z
      and u1' is not.  Contradiction => |W| <= 3, and |W| <= 2 when Q exists.
  => **d(P), d(Q) <= 7, and <= 5 whenever both slots are occupied**: ALL ELEVEN
     G21 SLOTS ARE NOW DEGREE-BOUNDED.  The mass budget sum_H (a-2) is therefore
     bounded, and Claim Q6.1 collapses to a FINITE WINDOW:
  E7  G31 (slot-wise a-caps, from the forced triangles inside each slot's
      neighbourhood): d(u1) = 2+[u1']+[A]+[P] while t(u1) >= [u1']+[A], so
      a(u1) <= 2 + [P]; symmetrically a(u2) <= 2+[Q], a(x) <= 2+[Q],
      a(y) <= 2+[P]; and u1',u2',A,B each carry a forced triangle (u~u1, z~u2,
      u1~u2, y~x respectively) so their a <= d-1 <= 4.
     both P,Q present  : sum_H(a-2) <= 21  => n <= 20
     P only            : sum_H(a-2) <= 18  => n <= 17
     Q only            : sum_H(a-2) <= 18  => n <= 17
     neither           : sum_H(a-2) <= 11  => n <= 10
     'neither' also gives H <= {u1',u2',A,B,h0}, i.e. |H| <= 5, contradicting
     |H| >= 6 (G18.1) -- so AT LEAST ONE opposite slot is occupied, and with
     n >= 14 (no C4-free graph with l > 3 on n <= 13):
     **14 <= n <= 20** (both) / **14 <= n <= 17** (exactly one).
  E8  G20 at x (if x is a hub, ecc(x) = 2): n = 1 + d(u)+d(y)+d(B)+d(Q) - 2t(x);
      with B absent this is n <= 1+3+3+5 = 12 < 14, so x hub => B present;
      symmetrically y hub => B present.
  => Claim Q6.1 is no longer an open class: it is a bounded, explicitly listed
     configuration problem with 14 <= n <= 20.  Round-8 target (NOT closed).

